Exception chaining — the cause.

Real code is layered. A low-level sequel-exception fires deep in a data-access method, but the caller of your service shouldn't have to know about SQL — they want a user-lookup-exception. The temptation is to catch the low-level one, and throw your own. But do it naively, and you destroy the evidence. Done right, exception chaining lets you translate the exception, and keep the original as its cause.

Here's the problem — a translated exception that loses the trail. You try to run a query. You catch the sequel-exception, and you throw a new user-lookup-exception saying "lookup failed". That reads cleanly at the service layer. But the sequel-exception — with the real reason, whether it's a timeout, a bad column, or a constraint, and with its stack trace — is gone. You've swapped a useful error for a vague one, and thrown away the debugging trail.

The fix is to pass the cause. Every exception can wrap another. You pass the original as a second constructor argument — the cause — and the chain is preserved. So you catch the sequel-exception as e, and you throw a new user-lookup-exception, with a message "lookup failed for" the id — and then e, as the cause. For this to work, your custom exception needs a constructor that accepts a throwable, and forwards it to super. So the constructor takes a message and a throwable cause, and calls super with both — and throwable stores the cause for you. You can also chain after the fact, with init-cause, but a cause-constructor is cleaner.

What do you get out of this? The "caused by" line in the stack trace. A chained exception prints both stories — your high-level message on top, then the original underneath, linked by the words "caused by". So at the top you see: user-lookup-exception, "lookup failed for forty-two", at user-service find. And below it: caused by, java sequel-exception, "connection timed out". You read it top-down, as a narrative: the lookup failed — because the SQL query failed — because the connection timed out. The abstraction stays clean at the top, and the root cause is one glance away. That's the whole payoff of chaining.

Now, the discipline of translation. Chaining is half of a broader habit sometimes called exception translation: you catch a low-level exception at a layer boundary, and re-throw one that fits the layer's vocabulary — carrying the cause. Two rules make it work. First, always pass the cause. Catching and re-throwing without the original is the single most common way real debugging information gets destroyed. If you catch-and-rethrow, chain — every time. Second, translate at boundaries, but don't over-wrap. Wrap when you're genuinely raising the abstraction level — data layer to service layer. Don't re-wrap the same exception at every frame; that just deepens the "caused by" ladder without adding meaning.

And finally, the anti-pattern to retire. The worst thing you can do to an exception is nothing — the swallowed exception. An empty catch block, catch sequel-exception, and then silence. The failure just vanished, with no trace at all. That turns a loud, traceable failure into an invisible one, and it is a leading cause of "impossible" bugs. At minimum, log it; better, handle it or chain it. The rule from section one holds all the way down: never let a failure pass silently.
